window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-cell","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Cell: The Unit of Life · Practice","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"New mitochondria in a cell arise by","image":"","options":["de novo assembly from cytoplasmic proteins","fission of pre-existing mitochondria","budding from the Golgi trans face","fusion of lysosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mitochondria divide by fission, so new ones come from pre-existing mitochondria. They are not formed from the Golgi or from lysosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The ribosomes of a polysome translate the same mRNA into proteins.","A prokaryotic ribosome measures about 15 nm by 20 nm.","Bacterial flagella have the same 9+2 microtubular axoneme as eukaryotic flagella.","Ribosomes in mitochondria and chloroplasts are 70S, like those of prokaryotes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bacterial flagella are structurally different from eukaryotic flagella. They consist of a filament, hook and basal body and lack the 9+2 microtubule axoneme. All the other statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A cell shows the following features under study: no membrane-bound nucleus, 70S ribosomes associated with the plasma membrane, cyanophycean granules lying free in the cytoplasm, gas vacuoles, and pigment-containing membranous extensions called chromatophores. The cell most likely belongs to:","image":"","options":["Mycoplasma","Chlamydomonas","A cyanobacterium (blue-green alga)","Euglena"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cyanophycean granules, gas vacuoles and pigment-bearing chromatophores together point to a cyanobacterium, which is a prokaryote. Chlamydomonas and Euglena are eukaryotes with nuclei, chloroplasts and 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes. Mycoplasma is not photosynthetic, so it lacks chromatophores.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about centrioles is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other.","Each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils, each of which is a triplet.","Each centriole has nine peripheral doublets surrounding a pair of central microtubules.","The proteinaceous hub in the proximal region is connected to the peripheral triplets by radial spokes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A centriole has a cartwheel organisation of nine peripheral triplets with a proteinaceous hub, and it has no central pair of microtubules. The 9 doublets + 2 central arrangement describes the axoneme of cilia and flagella, which is a common confusion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is an example of active transport across the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): In active transport, ions move along their concentration gradient without the use of ATP.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is indeed active transport. Active transport, however, moves substances against their concentration gradient (lower to higher) and uses ATP, so R describes passive movement and is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Golgi apparatus remains in close association with the endoplasmic reticulum.\nReason (R): Vesicles carrying materials from the ER fuse with the cis face of the Golgi apparatus, and the materials move towards its maturing face.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The Golgi receives ER-derived vesicles at its cis face and processes their contents as they move towards the trans face. This continuous supply from the ER explains why the two organelles lie close together.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A 70S ribosome is made of a 50S and a 30S subunit, even though 50 + 30 is not equal to 70.\nReason (R): 'S' (Svedberg unit) is a sedimentation coefficient that indirectly reflects density and size, so S values of subunits are not simply additive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The S value depends on how fast a particle sediments, which is governed by its size and density rather than plain mass. That is why the S values of the subunits do not add up arithmetically. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Many bacteria carry small circular DNA molecules in addition to their genomic DNA. Which unique phenotypic character is commonly conferred by this extra DNA?","image":"","options":["Ability to form a nuclear envelope","Resistance to antibiotics","Formation of 80S ribosomes","Ability to take up the Gram stain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Plasmids are small circular DNA molecules that lie outside the genomic DNA. They confer unique phenotypic characters, and antibiotic resistance is the example NCERT gives.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Schwann studied animal cells, reported a thin outer layer now known as the plasma membrane, and concluded that a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nStatement II: Rudolf Virchow explained that new cells are formed from pre-existing cells, and this gave the cell theory its final shape.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Schwann described the plasma membrane in animal cells and saw the cell wall as unique to plant cells. Virchow (1855) added 'Omnis cellula-e cellula', which completed the cell theory of Schleiden and Schwann.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a eukaryotic flagellum passes through its axoneme, and another section passes through the basal body from which it emerges. The total number of microtubules seen in the axoneme section and in the basal body section respectively would be","image":"","options":["20 and 27","18 and 27","20 and 29","11 and 9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The axoneme has a 9+2 array of nine doublets plus two central microtubules (9 × 2 + 2 = 20). The basal body is centriole-like, with nine triplets and only a proteinaceous hub at the centre (9 × 3 = 27). Adding a central pair to the basal body, which gives 29, is the key trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Nucleolus\n(B) Nuclear pore\n(C) Kinetochore\n(D) Satellite\nList-II\n(I) Disc-shaped structure on the sides of the centromere\n(II) Small chromosome fragment set off by a non-staining secondary constriction\n(III) Site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis\n(IV) Passage for two-way movement of RNA and proteins\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The nucleolus synthesises rRNA. Nuclear pores allow RNA and protein to move both ways. Kinetochores are discs flanking the centromere. A satellite is the fragment beyond a secondary constriction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria are not easily visible under the light microscope unless specifically stained.\nReason (R): Mitochondria lack their own DNA and ribosomes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that mitochondria are not easily seen unless specifically stained, so A is true. R is false because mitochondria contain circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Simple diffusion\n(B) Osmosis\n(C) Active transport\n(D) Carrier-assisted passive transport\nList-II\n(i) Movement of water by diffusion\n(ii) Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump using ATP\n(iii) Neutral solutes moving along the concentration gradient\n(iv) Polar molecules crossing with the help of a membrane protein, without energy expenditure\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Neutral solutes cross by simple diffusion, and water moves by osmosis. The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump uses ATP to move ions against their gradient (active transport). Polar molecules cross passively with a carrier protein.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a prokaryotic cell, the ribosomes are associated with the:","image":"","options":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Nuclear envelope","Plasma membrane","Golgi apparatus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Prokaryotes have no ER, nuclear envelope or Golgi apparatus, so their ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane. Ribosomes on the ER and on the nuclear envelope are features of eukaryotic cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A chromosome whose centromere is located at its very end is called:","image":"","options":["Acrocentric","Sub-metacentric","Metacentric","Telocentric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A telocentric chromosome has a terminal centromere. An acrocentric chromosome has its centromere close to, but not at, the end, giving one extremely short arm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Amyloplast\n(B) Aleuroplast\n(C) Chromoplast\n(D) Chloroplast\nList-II\n(I) Stores proteins\n(II) Contains chlorophyll and carotenoids that trap light energy\n(III) Stores starch, e.g., in potato\n(IV) Contains fat-soluble carotene and xanthophylls giving yellow, orange or red colour\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amyloplasts store starch (as in potato), aleuroplasts store proteins and chromoplasts carry carotene and xanthophylls. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll and carotenoids for photosynthesis. Do not confuse the 'amylo' (starch) and 'aleuro' (protein) prefixes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (bacterial structure) with List-II (feature/function):\nList-I\nA. Glycocalyx\nB. Cell wall\nC. Mesosome\nD. Chromatophore\nList-II\n(i) Determines cell shape and prevents the bacterium from bursting or collapsing\n(ii) Pigment-containing membranous extension seen in cyanobacteria\n(iii) Outermost layer of the cell envelope; may be a slime layer or a capsule\n(iv) Infolding of plasma membrane that increases its surface area and enzymatic content\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The glycocalyx is the outermost envelope layer (slime layer or capsule), and the cell wall gives shape and structural support. Mesosomes are membrane infoldings that increase surface area and enzymatic content. Chromatophores carry pigments in cyanobacteria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not considered part of the endomembrane system.\nReason (R): Mitochondria and chloroplasts are not bound by membranes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true, but the reason is that their functions are not coordinated with the ER, Golgi, lysosomes and vacuoles. R is false because both organelles are double membrane-bound.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Scientist)\nA. Matthias Schleiden\nB. Theodore Schwann\nC. Rudolf Virchow\nD. Robert Brown\nList-II (Contribution)\nI. Discovered the nucleus\nII. New cells are formed from pre-existing cells\nIII. All plants are composed of different kinds of cells that form tissues\nIV. Concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schleiden (botanist, 1838) found that plants are made of different kinds of cells forming tissues. Schwann (1839) concluded that the cell wall is unique to plant cells, Virchow (1855) proposed that cells arise from pre-existing cells, and Brown discovered the nucleus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about lysosomes are correct?\n(i) They are formed by the process of packaging in the Golgi apparatus.\n(ii) They are rich in lipases, proteases and carbohydrases.\n(iii) Their enzymes work best at alkaline pH.\n(iv) They are membrane-bound vesicular structures.\n(v) Their enzymes can digest nucleic acids.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (v) are correct, making four. Statement (iii) is wrong because lysosomal hydrolases are optimally active at acidic pH, not alkaline pH.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A prokaryote is found to lack the middle layer of the typical three-layered bacterial cell envelope. Which of the following is most consistent with NCERT?","image":"","options":["It must be a cyanobacterium, since chromatophores replace the cell wall","It is likely a mycoplasma, and it lacks the structure that determines cell shape and protects against bursting","It must lack a plasma membrane as well, since the envelope layers act as a single unit","It will necessarily be Gram positive because the stain binds directly to the membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The middle layer of the envelope is the cell wall, and mycoplasma is the only prokaryote that lacks one. The cell wall determines shape and prevents bursting, so without it the cell loses that support. The plasma membrane is still present.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements.\nStatement I: Based on his studies of plant tissues, Schwann concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nStatement II: Rudolf Virchow explained that new cells are formed from pre-existing cells (Omnis cellula-e cellula).\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Schwann did conclude that the cell wall is unique to plant cells. Virchow (1855) added that cells arise from pre-existing cells, which completed the cell theory. Both statements are true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\n(a) Mitochondrial matrix\n(b) Chloroplast stroma\n(c) Rough ER of an animal cell\n(d) Cytoplasm of a bacterial cell\n(e) Cytoplasm of a yeast cell","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The mitochondrial matrix, the chloroplast stroma and the bacterial cytoplasm have 70S ribosomes. The rough ER of animal cells and the cytoplasm of yeast, which is a eukaryote, carry 80S ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The primary wall of a young plant cell is capable of growth.\nReason (R): The secondary wall is laid down on the outer side of the primary wall as the cell matures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The primary wall of a young cell can grow, and this capacity gradually decreases as the cell matures. However, the secondary wall forms on the inner side, towards the membrane, not the outer side, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A human red blood cell is about 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter, and a Mycoplasma cell is about 0.3 $\\mu m$ long. Roughly how many Mycoplasma cells, placed end to end, would span the diameter of one RBC?","image":"","options":["About 2","About 23","About 230","About 2300"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"7.0 $\\mu m$ / 0.3 $\\mu m$ is about 23. Both values are already in micrometres, so no conversion is needed. Treating 0.3 as nanometres or millimetres gives the wrong orders of magnitude.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements comparing mitochondria and chloroplasts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Both are double membrane-bound and possess circular DNA and 70S ribosomes","The number of chloroplasts ranges from one per cell in Chlamydomonas to 20-40 per mesophyll cell","In mitochondria, the inner compartment enclosed by the inner membrane is filled with the matrix, while the outer compartment lies between the two membranes","The inner membrane of the chloroplast folds into cristae that bear chlorophyll pigments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane, not of the chloroplast. In chloroplasts, chlorophyll lies on the thylakoids found in the stroma. All the other statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Different cells have different sizes. Arrange the following cells in an ascending order of their size. Choose the correct option among the followings\ni. Mycoplasma\nii. Ostrich eggs\niii. Human RBC\niv. Bacteria","image":"","options":["i, iv, iii & ii","i, iii, iv & ii","ii, i, iii & iv","iii, ii, i & iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mycoplasma is the smallest cell at about 0.3 µm. Typical bacteria are 3–5 µm and a human RBC is about 7 µm. The ostrich egg is the largest single cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump in the plasma membrane is an example of:","image":"","options":["Simple diffusion","Osmosis","Passive transport through a carrier protein","Active transport using ATP"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions against their concentration gradient. This uses ATP, so it is active transport. Carrier-mediated movement down a gradient, by contrast, is passive.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Some bacteria attach to rocks in streams and to host tissues using small bristle-like fibres that sprout out of the cell. These fibres are:","image":"","options":["Flagella","Mesosomes","Fimbriae","Chromatophores"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fimbriae are small bristle-like fibres that help some bacteria attach to rocks in streams and to host tissues. Flagella are for motility. Mesosomes and chromatophores are internal membrane extensions, not surface fibres.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In plant cells, the concentration of many ions is significantly higher in the vacuole than in the surrounding cytoplasm.\nStatement II: Ions enter the plant vacuole by simple diffusion across the tonoplast, without any expenditure of energy.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The tonoplast moves ions into the vacuole against their concentration gradient, which is why they become more concentrated there. Movement against a gradient is active transport and needs energy, so simple diffusion cannot explain it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Amyloplasts\n(B) Elaioplasts\n(C) Aleuroplasts\n(D) Chromoplasts\nList-II\n(I) Store proteins\n(II) Contain fat-soluble carotenoid pigments\n(III) Store starch\n(IV) Store oils and fats\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amyloplasts store starch (for example in potato), elaioplasts store oils and fats, and aleuroplasts store proteins. Chromoplasts carry carotenoids such as carotene and xanthophylls.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The phrase 'Omnis cellula-e cellula', meaning that new cells arise only from pre-existing cells, was given by:","image":"","options":["Matthias Schleiden","Theodore Schwann","Rudolf Virchow","Robert Brown"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In 1855, Rudolf Virchow explained that cells divide and that new cells form from pre-existing cells. Schleiden and Schwann had proposed the cell theory but did not explain how new cells arise.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about prokaryotic cells:\n(a) Mycoplasma is the prokaryote that lacks a cell wall.\n(b) Inclusion bodies such as glycogen granules are enclosed by a single membrane.\n(c) In cyanobacteria, chromatophores are pigment-containing membranous extensions into the cytoplasm.\n(d) The bacterial flagellum has a filament, a hook and a basal body, and the filament is its longest part.\n(e) Plasmid DNA is separated from the cytoplasm by a nuclear membrane.\nChoose the option with only the correct statements.","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(a) and (d) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (d) are correct. Inclusion bodies lie free in the cytoplasm without any membrane, so (b) is false. Prokaryotes have no nuclear membrane at all, so (e) is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a squash of a dividing cell, a student observes a chromosome with the following features:\n- its primary constriction lies slightly away from the middle, giving one shorter and one longer arm;\n- near the tip of one arm, a non-staining constriction at a constant location makes a small terminal fragment appear separate.\nWhich interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["Sub-metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a satellite formed due to a secondary constriction","Acrocentric chromosome; the small fragment is the kinetochore","Metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a satellite formed due to the primary constriction","Sub-metacentric chromosome; the small fragment is a nucleolus attached to the arm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A slightly off-centre centromere means the chromosome is sub-metacentric. A non-staining secondary constriction at a constant location produces the satellite. Kinetochores are disc-shaped structures on the sides of the centromere (the primary constriction), not terminal fragments.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Vesicles carrying materials from the ER first fuse with which face of the Golgi apparatus, and what is its shape?","image":"","options":["Trans face, which is concave","Trans face, which is convex","Cis face, which is concave","Cis face, which is convex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"ER-derived vesicles fuse with the cis face, also called the forming face, which is convex. The trans face is the concave maturing face from which materials leave.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Besides polysaccharides, the cell walls of some algae contain which mineral?","image":"","options":["Calcium pectate","Sodium chloride","Potassium nitrate","Calcium carbonate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Algal cell walls contain cellulose, galactans, mannans and minerals such as calcium carbonate. Calcium pectate is the main component of the middle lamella, not a mineral deposit of the algal wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitochondria are called the 'power houses' of the cell.\nReason (R): Mitochondria are the sites of aerobic respiration and produce cellular energy in the form of ATP.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mitochondria are named power houses precisely because aerobic respiration in them generates ATP. So the reason directly explains the assertion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Contractile vacuole\n(B) Food vacuole\n(C) Lysosome\n(D) Golgi apparatus\nList-II\n(I) Hydrolases optimally active at acidic pH\n(II) Formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids\n(III) Osmoregulation and excretion in Amoeba\n(IV) Formed by engulfing food particles in protists","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The contractile vacuole of Amoeba handles osmoregulation and excretion, and food vacuoles form when food particles are engulfed. Lysosomes hold acid-active hydrolases, and the Golgi is the key site for forming glycoproteins and glycolipids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell) with List-II (size feature) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\nA. Mycoplasma\nB. Bacterium\nC. Human red blood cell\nD. Nerve cell\nList-II:\nI. About 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter\nII. About 0.3 $\\mu m$ in length\nIII. 3 to 5 $\\mu m$\nIV. Among the longest cells","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mycoplasma is the smallest cell at about 0.3 $\\mu m$. Bacteria can be 3 to 5 $\\mu m$, human RBCs are about 7.0 $\\mu m$ across, and nerve cells are among the longest cells. A common trap is swapping the Mycoplasma and bacterium sizes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Polar molecules require a carrier protein of the membrane to be transported across the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): Polar molecules cannot pass through the nonpolar lipid bilayer.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The hydrophobic interior of the bilayer blocks polar molecules, so they need carrier proteins to cross. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cilia work like oars and can move either the cell or the fluid surrounding it.\nStatement II: Flagella of prokaryotic bacteria are structurally identical to eukaryotic flagella.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cilia beat like oars and move either the cell or the surrounding fluid. Bacterial flagella do exist, but they are structurally different from eukaryotic 9+2 flagella, so Statement II is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The movement of water across the plasma membrane by diffusion, from a region of its higher concentration to a region of its lower concentration, is called:","image":"","options":["Active transport","Osmosis","Endocytosis","Facilitated transport"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Osmosis is the diffusion of water across the membrane down its concentration gradient. It is passive and needs no ATP.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about prokaryotic cells is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells","Prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and are made of 50S and 30S subunits","Inclusion bodies such as glycogen and phosphate granules are enclosed by a single membrane","In cyanobacteria, chromatophores are membranous extensions into the cytoplasm that contain pigments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Inclusion bodies store reserve material and are not bound by any membrane system; they lie free in the cytoplasm. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Chromatin contains DNA, histones, some non-histone proteins and RNA.\n(ii) A single human cell has roughly two metres of DNA distributed among its 46 chromosomes.\n(iii) Kinetochores can be clearly seen in the chromatin of an interphase nucleus.\n(iv) Secondary constrictions are non-staining and occur at a constant location.\n(v) Microbodies are non-membrane bound granules.\n(vi) Microbodies occur only in plant cells.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. Chromosomes, and hence kinetochores, are visible only in dividing cells, not in the loose interphase chromatin. Microbodies are membrane-bound vesicles found in both plant and animal cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT considered part of the endomembrane system?","image":"","options":["Lysosome","Golgi complex","Peroxisome","Vacuole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system includes the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles because their functions are coordinated. Peroxisomes, like mitochondria and chloroplasts, are membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with these organelles, so they are excluded.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A sectional diagram of a chloroplast shows three labelled regions:\nX – the space bounded by the inner membrane\nY – flattened membranous sacs piled like stacks of coins\nZ – flat membranous tubules joining the stacks Y to one another\nWhich of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["X contains enzymes needed for synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins","X contains small double-stranded circular DNA molecules","The ribosomes present in X are of the 80S type, like cytoplasmic ribosomes","Y are the grana and Z are the stroma lamellae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"X is the stroma, which contains enzymes, circular DNA and 70S ribosomes; these are smaller than the 80S cytoplasmic ribosomes. Y are grana and Z are stroma lamellae, so the only incorrect statement is the one about 80S ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Membrane proteins are classified as integral or peripheral on the basis of how easily they can be extracted from the membrane.\nStatement II: Peripheral proteins are totally buried within the lipid bilayer, whereas integral proteins lie only on its surface.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The classification is indeed based on ease of extraction. However, Statement II reverses the definitions: peripheral proteins lie on the surface, while integral proteins are partially or totally buried.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the centrosome and centrioles are correct?\n(i) The two centrioles of a centrosome lie parallel to each other.\n(ii) Each centriole has nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin, each fibril being a triplet.\n(iii) Centrioles are surrounded by amorphous pericentriolar material.\n(iv) In animal cells, centrioles give rise to the spindle fibres during cell division.\n(v) Adjacent peripheral triplets of a centriole are not linked to each other.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (ii), (iii) and (iv) are correct. The two centrioles lie perpendicular to each other, not parallel, and adjacent triplets are linked, so (i) and (v) are wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rudolf Virchow modified the hypothesis of Schleiden and Schwann and gave the cell theory its final shape.\nReason (R): The cell theory formulated by Schleiden and Schwann did not explain how new cells are formed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The original theory was silent on how new cells originate. Virchow filled this gap by showing that cells come from pre-existing cells, which completed the theory, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Anything less than a complete structure of a cell does not ensure independent living.\n(b) As originally formulated by Schleiden and Schwann, the cell theory explained how new cells are formed.\n(c) According to the cell theory as understood today, living organisms are composed of cells and products of cells.\n(d) The cell is the structural, but not the functional, unit of living organisms.\n(e) The invention of the microscope and its improvement leading to the electron microscope revealed the structural details of the cell.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (e) are correct. Statement (b) is wrong because the origin of new cells was explained only later by Virchow. Statement (d) is wrong because the cell is both the structural and the functional unit of life.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the longest portion of a bacterial flagellum, extending from the cell surface to the outside?","image":"","options":["Basal body","Hook","Filament","Pilus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bacterial flagellum has three parts: filament, hook and basal body. The filament is the longest part and projects outward from the cell surface. The basal body anchors the flagellum, and a pilus is a separate surface structure, not part of the flagellum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a mesophyll cell of a green leaf, identify the ribosome type found at each location in the order: free in cytoplasm, mitochondrial matrix, chloroplast stroma, attached to rough ER.","image":"","options":["80S, 80S, 70S, 80S","80S, 70S, 70S, 80S","70S, 70S, 70S, 80S","80S, 70S, 80S, 70S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cytoplasmic and RER-bound ribosomes of eukaryotes are 80S. Mitochondria and chloroplasts carry their own smaller 70S ribosomes. The common trap is assuming that every ribosome inside a eukaryotic cell is 80S.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Mesosome\n(B) Tonoplast\n(C) Kinetochore\n(D) Hub\nList-II\n(i) Disc-shaped structure on the sides of the centromere\n(ii) Proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole\n(iii) Infolding of plasma membrane in prokaryotes\n(iv) Single membrane bounding the vacuole\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The mesosome is a prokaryotic infolding of the plasma membrane, and the tonoplast is the membrane around the vacuole. Kinetochores are discs on either side of the centromere, and the hub is the proteinaceous centre of a centriole joined to the triplets by radial spokes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Membranous extensions into the cytoplasm that contain pigments are found in prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria. What are these structures called?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes","Inclusion bodies","Chromatophores","Fimbriae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chromatophores are pigment-containing membranous extensions into the cytoplasm, found in prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria. Mesosomes are also membrane infoldings, but they are not described as pigment-bearing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed by NCERT as constituents of chromatin?\nDNA, histones, non-histone proteins, RNA, cellulose, calcium pectate","image":"","options":["3","5","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Chromatin contains DNA, basic histone proteins, some non-histone proteins and RNA, which makes four. Cellulose and calcium pectate are cell wall and middle lamella components.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The two subunits of a eukaryotic 80S ribosome are:","image":"","options":["50S and 30S","60S and 20S","60S and 40S","40S and 30S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An 80S ribosome has a larger 60S subunit and a smaller 40S subunit. The 50S and 30S pair makes up the prokaryotic 70S ribosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following mature cells lacks a nucleus?","image":"","options":["Mesophyll cell of a leaf","Sieve tube cell of a vascular plant","Columnar epithelial cell","White blood cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the sieve tube cells of vascular plants and the erythrocytes of many mammals as mature cells without a nucleus. The other three cells keep their nuclei.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of the two membranes of the chloroplast, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable.\nStatement II: Stroma lamellae are flat membranous tubules that connect thylakoids of different grana.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the inner chloroplast membrane is relatively less permeable. It also describes stroma lamellae as flat tubules linking the thylakoids of different grana, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about prokaryotic cells:\nStatement I: Pili and fimbriae are surface structures of bacteria that help in motility, and fimbriae may help bacteria attach to rocks in streams.\nStatement II: Inclusion bodies such as phosphate granules, cyanophycean granules and glycogen granules lie free in the cytoplasm and are not bound by any membrane system.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pili and fimbriae are bacterial surface structures, but they play no role in motility; only flagella move the cell. So Statement I is wrong, even though its point about fimbriae attaching to rocks and host tissues is true. Statement II is correct: reserve-material inclusion bodies are not membrane bound and lie free in the cytoplasm.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.\nStatement II: The centrosome of animal cells is a membrane bound organelle that helps in cell division.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ribosomes lack a membrane and occur in all cells, so Statement I is correct. The centrosome does help in cell division, but it is a non-membrane bound organelle, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A section passes through the common boundary of two adjacent mature plant cells, P and Q, at a point where there are no plasmodesmata. Which sequence correctly gives the layers met while moving from the cytoplasm of cell P to the cytoplasm of cell Q?","image":"","options":["Plasma membrane (P) → primary wall (P) → secondary wall (P) → middle lamella → secondary wall (Q) → primary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)","Primary wall (P) → plasma membrane (P) → middle lamella → plasma membrane (Q) → primary wall (Q)","Plasma membrane (P) → middle lamella → secondary wall (P) → primary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)","Plasma membrane (P) → secondary wall (P) → primary wall (P) → middle lamella → primary wall (Q) → secondary wall (Q) → plasma membrane (Q)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The secondary wall lies inside the primary wall, next to the membrane, and the middle lamella joins the primary walls of the two cells. The common trap is placing the primary wall next to the membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Capsule\nB. Cell wall\nC. Plasma membrane\nD. Mesosome\nList-II\ni. Selectively permeable layer that interacts with the outside world\nii. Infolding of membrane that helps in respiration and DNA replication\niii. Determines cell shape and prevents the bacterium from bursting or collapsing\niv. Thick and tough form of glycocalyx\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The capsule is the tough form of glycocalyx, and the cell wall gives shape and structural support. The plasma membrane is selectively permeable, and the mesosome is a membrane infolding that helps in respiration and DNA replication.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are present in the mitochondrial matrix?\n(i) Single circular DNA molecule\n(ii) A few RNA molecules\n(iii) 70S ribosomes\n(iv) Components required for protein synthesis\n(v) 80S ribosomes\n(vi) Chlorophyll","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Five","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The matrix contains a single circular DNA, a few RNAs, 70S ribosomes and the components needed for protein synthesis, which makes four items. Its ribosomes are 70S rather than 80S, and chlorophyll occurs only in chloroplasts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about prokaryotic cells are correct?\n(a) Mesosomes help in respiration and secretion processes.\n(b) The genetic material of prokaryotes is enclosed by a nuclear membrane.\n(c) Plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA.\n(d) Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall around its cell membrane.\n(e) The three layers of the bacterial cell envelope function independently and do not act as a single protective unit.\n(f) Bacteria that take up the Gram stain are called Gram positive.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c), (d) and (f) are correct. Statement (b) is wrong because prokaryotic DNA is naked, with no nuclear envelope. Statement (e) is wrong because the glycocalyx, cell wall and plasma membrane each have distinct functions but act together as one protective unit.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical bacterial cell envelope, which sequence correctly lists the three layers from the outside inward?","image":"","options":["Cell wall → glycocalyx → plasma membrane","Glycocalyx → cell wall → plasma membrane","Plasma membrane → cell wall → glycocalyx","Glycocalyx → plasma membrane → cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The bacterial cell envelope is a tightly bound three-layered structure. The glycocalyx is outermost, the cell wall lies beneath it, and the plasma membrane is innermost. Placing the cell wall outside the glycocalyx is the common error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nucleus is correct?","image":"","options":["The nucleolus is a membrane-bound structure and is the site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis","Nuclear pores are formed by fusion of the two nuclear membranes and allow movement of RNA and proteins in both directions","Mature sieve tube cells and erythrocytes of most mammals possess a single prominent nucleus","The outer nuclear membrane is usually continuous with the Golgi apparatus and lacks ribosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nuclear pores form where the two membranes of the envelope fuse, and they allow two-way traffic of RNA and proteins. The nucleolus is not membrane-bound, and mature sieve tube cells and most mammalian RBCs lack a nucleus. The outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the ER and bears ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT included in the endomembrane system, because its functions are not coordinated with those of the ER and Golgi complex?","image":"","options":["Lysosome","Vacuole","Peroxisome","Golgi complex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system includes the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles. Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane-bound, but they are excluded because their functions are not coordinated with these organelles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Select one which is not true for ribosome","image":"","options":["Made of two sub-units","Form polysome","May attach to mRNA","Have no role in protein synthesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Ribosomes have two subunits and attach to mRNA. Several ribosomes on one mRNA form a polysome. Their main job is protein synthesis, so saying they have no role in it is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the axoneme of a eukaryotic cilium or flagellum are correct?\n(i) It has nine doublets of radially arranged peripheral microtubules.\n(ii) It has a pair of centrally located microtubules connected by bridges.\n(iii) The central sheath is connected to one tubule of each peripheral doublet by a radial spoke.\n(iv) The peripheral microtubules are present as triplets linked to a central hub.\n(v) The peripheral doublets are interconnected by linkers.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) describe the 9+2 axoneme correctly. Statement (iv) describes the centriole, which has peripheral triplets and a hub, not the axoneme.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A piece of plant tissue is treated with a reagent that selectively dissolves calcium pectate and leaves cellulose untouched. What is the most likely immediate result?","image":"","options":["Individual cells lose their shape and burst, because the cellulose wall is digested","Neighbouring cells separate from one another, but each cell keeps its own wall","The plasma membrane of each cell is dissolved, releasing the cytoplasm","Chloroplast grana break down, because their thylakoids are made of pectin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Calcium pectate is the main component of the middle lamella, which glues adjacent cells together. Dissolving it frees the cells from one another, while their cellulose-rich walls remain intact and maintain shape.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A protein meant for secretion is made on ribosomes attached to the ER and then modified before it leaves the cell. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["SER → trans face of Golgi → cisternae → cis face → secretory vesicle","RER → vesicle → trans face of Golgi → cisternae → cis face → secretory vesicle","RER → vesicle → cis face of Golgi → cisternae → trans face → secretory vesicle","Nucleolus → RER → lysosome → cis face of Golgi → secretory vesicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Proteins made on the RER travel in vesicles that fuse with the convex cis (forming) face. They are modified in the cisternae and released from the concave trans (maturing) face. The trap is reversing the cis and trans faces, or starting at the SER, which lacks ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of chromosome) with List-II (position of centromere):\nList-I\nA. Metacentric\nB. Sub-metacentric\nC. Acrocentric\nD. Telocentric\nList-II\nI. Terminal centromere\nII. Centromere close to one end, giving one extremely short and one very long arm\nIII. Centromere in the middle, giving two equal arms\nIV. Centromere slightly away from the middle, giving one shorter and one longer arm","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The centromere is in the middle in metacentric chromosomes and slightly off-centre in sub-metacentric ones. It lies near the end in acrocentric chromosomes and is terminal in telocentric ones. The usual trap is confusing acrocentric (near the end) with telocentric (at the end).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The endoplasmic reticulum divides the intracellular space into luminal and extra-luminal (cytoplasmic) compartments.","Rough ER is continuous with the outer membrane of the nucleus.","The cis and trans faces of the Golgi apparatus are entirely different but interconnected.","Mitochondria and chloroplasts are excluded from the endomembrane system because they are not bounded by membranes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are double-membrane-bound organelles. They are left out of the endomembrane system because their functions are not coordinated with the ER, Golgi, lysosomes and vacuoles, not because they lack membranes. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cell components are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nGolgi complex, mitochondrion, lysosome, peroxisome, smooth endoplasmic reticulum, vacuole, chloroplast, ribosome","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system includes the ER (smooth ER here), Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles, which gives four. Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane bound, but their functions are not coordinated with these organelles, so they are excluded. Ribosomes have no membrane at all.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the Golgi apparatus are correct?\n(i) Densely stained reticular structures near the nucleus were first observed by Camillo Golgi in 1898.\n(ii) Its cisternae are flat, disc-shaped sacs of about 0.5 µm to 1.0 µm diameter.\n(iii) The cis face is concave and is called the maturing face.\n(iv) The cis and trans faces are entirely different but interconnected.\n(v) Proteins synthesised on ER-bound ribosomes are modified in Golgi cisternae before release from the trans face.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iv) and (v) are correct. Statement (iii) is wrong because the cis face is convex and is the forming face, while the concave trans face is the maturing face.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"What is a tonoplast?","image":"","options":["Outer membrane of mitochondria","Inner membrane of chloroplast","Membrane boundary of the vacuole of plant cells","Cell membrane of a plant cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The tonoplast is the single membrane surrounding the vacuole in plant cells. It actively transports ions and other materials into the vacuole.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In prokaryotes, the genetic material is basically naked and is not enveloped by a nuclear membrane.\nStatement II: Plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Prokaryotes have no well-defined nucleus, so their genomic DNA lies naked in the cytoplasm. NCERT also states that plasmid DNA is used to monitor bacterial transformation with foreign DNA, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nucleus was first described as a cell organelle in 1831 by one scientist. A second scientist later named the nuclear material that stains with basic dyes 'chromatin'. Who were these two scientists, in that order?","image":"","options":["Antonie von Leeuwenhoek; Rudolf Virchow","Robert Brown; Flemming","Robert Brown; Camillo Golgi","Matthias Schleiden; Flemming"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Robert Brown described the nucleus in 1831. Flemming later named the basic-dye-stained nuclear material chromatin. Leeuwenhoek first saw a live cell and Golgi described Golgi bodies, so those pairings are wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Vesicles carrying materials from the ER fuse with the concave trans face of the Golgi apparatus and move towards the convex cis face.\nStatement II: The Golgi apparatus is an important site for the formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Vesicles from the ER fuse with the convex cis (forming) face and move towards the concave trans (maturing) face, so Statement I reverses the direction. Statement II is correct as given in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about prokaryotic cells is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Mesosomes help in cell wall formation, DNA replication and distribution to daughter cells","Inclusion bodies such as glycogen and phosphate granules are enclosed by a single membrane","Plasmid DNA can confer resistance to antibiotics","Prokaryotic ribosomes are associated with the plasma membrane and measure about 15 nm by 20 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inclusion bodies are not bound by any membrane system and lie free in the cytoplasm. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"As a plant cell matures, its secondary wall is deposited:","image":"","options":["On the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane","On the outer side of the middle lamella","Between the primary wall and the middle lamella","Inside the cytoplasm as a separate layer around the vacuole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The secondary wall forms on the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane. Because of this, the primary wall stays outermost next to the middle lamella.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Rough endoplasmic reticulum is extensive in cells that actively synthesise and secrete proteins.\nReason (R): Rough endoplasmic reticulum bears ribosomes, the sites of protein synthesis, on its outer surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The RER carries ribosomes on its outer surface, and ribosomes make proteins. This is exactly why the RER is abundant in cells engaged in protein synthesis and secretion, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some bacteria the glycocalyx is thick and tough rather than a loose sheath. What is this thick, tough glycocalyx called?","image":"","options":["Slime layer","Mesosome","Capsule","Chromatophore"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A thick, tough glycocalyx is called a capsule. A loose glycocalyx sheath is called the slime layer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\nCytoplasm of a bacterium, Mitochondrial matrix of a liver cell, Stroma of a chloroplast, Rough ER of a pancreatic cell, Cytoplasm of a cyanobacterium, Free cytoplasm of a yeast cell","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four locations have 70S ribosomes: bacteria and cyanobacteria (prokaryotes), plus mitochondria and chloroplasts, which carry their own 70S ribosomes. Ribosomes on the RER and in yeast cytoplasm are eukaryotic 80S ribosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Lysosomes, Chloroplast, Vacuoles, Peroxisomes","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Seven"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles count, because their functions are coordinated. Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane-bound but are excluded since their functions are not coordinated with these organelles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some bacteria the outermost layer of the cell envelope, the glycocalyx, is thick and tough. This form of glycocalyx is called the:","image":"","options":["Slime layer","Capsule","Mesosome","Middle lamella"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A thick, tough glycocalyx is a capsule. A loose sheath is called a slime layer, which is the common confusion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The content of the nucleolus is continuous with the rest of the nucleoplasm.\nReason (R): The nucleolus is not a membrane-bound structure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Because no membrane surrounds the nucleolus, its contents merge directly with the nucleoplasm. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is not true of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Cell wall is made up of peptidoglycans","80S type of ribosomes are present in the cytoplasm","Mitochondria contain circular DNA","Membrane bound organelles are present"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Peptidoglycan cell walls are characteristic of bacteria. Eukaryotic cell walls are made of materials such as cellulose (plants) or chitin (fungi).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The name 'chromatin' was given to the material of the nucleus that is stained by basic dyes. Who gave it this name?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown","Flemming","Camillo Golgi","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Flemming named the basic-dye-staining nuclear material chromatin. Robert Brown described the nucleus itself in 1831, which is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A cell is treated with a poison that completely stops ATP production, while the membrane itself stays intact. Which of the following processes would be directly stopped?","image":"","options":["Diffusion of a neutral solute along its concentration gradient","Osmotic movement of water from higher to lower water concentration","Carrier protein-mediated passage of a polar molecule down its concentration gradient","Pumping of Na$^+$ ions from a region of lower to higher concentration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Only transport against a concentration gradient depends on ATP, as with the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump. Diffusion, osmosis and carrier-mediated movement down a gradient are passive, so they continue without ATP.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Plastid differs from mitochondria on the basis of one of the following features. Mark the right answer.","image":"","options":["Presence of two layers of membrane","Presence of ribosome","Presence of thylakoids","Presence of DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plastids and mitochondria both have a double membrane, their own DNA and 70S ribosomes. Thylakoids are found only in plastids (chloroplasts).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Plasmodesmata are best described as:","image":"","options":["Infoldings of the plasma membrane that increase its surface area","Pores in the nuclear envelope that allow RNA to move out","Connections through the cell wall and middle lamella that link the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells","Thickenings of the secondary wall that give mechanical strength"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plasmodesmata pass through the cell wall and middle lamella and join the cytoplasm of adjacent plant cells. Membrane infoldings in prokaryotes are mesosomes, and nuclear pores are a different structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A prokaryotic ribosome made of a 50S and a 30S subunit is called a 70S ribosome and not an 80S ribosome.\nReason (R): 'S' is the Svedberg unit of sedimentation coefficient, which is indirectly a measure of density and size, so subunit S values do not simply add up.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The S value reflects how fast a particle sediments, which depends on its density and size, not on simple mass addition. That is why 50S + 30S gives a 70S particle. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Chromoplasts impart yellow, orange or red colour to some parts of a plant.\nReason (R): Chromoplasts contain water-soluble pigments such as chlorophyll dissolved in the stroma.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chromoplasts give yellow, orange or red colours because they contain fat-soluble carotenoid pigments such as carotene and xanthophylls. They do not contain water-soluble chlorophyll, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Cytoplasm is the main arena of cellular activities in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells","In a eukaryotic cell, ribosomes occur in the cytoplasm, on rough ER, and inside mitochondria and chloroplasts","The centrosome of animal cells, which helps in cell division, is a single-membrane bound organelle","Nerve cells are some of the longest cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The centrosome is a non-membrane bound organelle. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plastid) with List-II (content):\nList-I\n(a) Amyloplast\n(b) Elaioplast\n(c) Aleuroplast\n(d) Chromoplast\nList-II\n(i) Proteins\n(ii) Fat-soluble carotenoids such as xanthophylls\n(iii) Starch\n(iv) Oils and fats","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amyloplasts store starch (as in potato), elaioplasts store oils and fats, and aleuroplasts store proteins. Chromoplasts carry carotenoid pigments. The common trap is swapping elaioplasts and aleuroplasts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In Amoeba, the contractile vacuole is mainly important for:","image":"","options":["Digestion of engulfed food particles","Osmoregulation and excretion","Storage of starch","Synthesis of lipids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The contractile vacuole of Amoeba carries out osmoregulation and excretion. Digestion of engulfed particles takes place in food vacuoles, which is a different structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements about the Golgi apparatus.\nStatement I: The cis face of the Golgi apparatus is concave and is called the maturing face.\nStatement II: The Golgi apparatus is an important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is wrong because the cis face is convex and is the forming face; the concave trans face is the maturing face. Statement II is correct as stated in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The arrangement of microtubules in the axoneme of a typical eukaryotic cilium or flagellum is described as:","image":"","options":["9 + 0, with nine peripheral triplets","9 + 2, with nine peripheral doublets and a central pair","9 + 2, with nine peripheral triplets and a central pair","9 + 1, with nine peripheral doublets and one central tubule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The axoneme has nine radially arranged peripheral doublets and two central microtubules, the 9+2 array. Triplets are found in centrioles, not in the axoneme.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The hydrolytic enzymes present in lysosomes are optimally active at:","image":"","options":["Acidic pH","Neutral pH","Alkaline pH","Any pH between 2 and 12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lysosomal hydrolases, such as lipases, proteases and carbohydrases, work best at an acidic pH.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A colourless plastid isolated from the seed of an oil-yielding plant is found to store oils and fats. This plastid is a/an","image":"","options":["amyloplast","aleuroplast","elaioplast","chromoplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Elaioplasts are leucoplasts that store oils and fats. Amyloplasts store starch and aleuroplasts store proteins, while chromoplasts are coloured plastids, not colourless ones.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"New cells generate from","image":"","options":["bacterial fermentation","regeneration of old cells","pre-existing cells","abiotic materials"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rudolf Virchow stated 'Omnis cellula-e cellula', meaning all cells arise from pre-existing cells. This principle completed the modern cell theory.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The layer that cements neighbouring plant cells together is chiefly made of which substance?","image":"","options":["Cellulose","Calcium pectate","Calcium carbonate","Hemicellulose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The middle lamella holds adjacent plant cells together and is made mainly of calcium pectate. Calcium carbonate is a mineral found in some algal cell walls, not the cementing layer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the cytoskeleton of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Microtubules","Microfilaments","Intermediate filaments","Cisternae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The cytoskeleton is a network of proteinaceous microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments. Cisternae are flattened membranous sacs of the ER and Golgi apparatus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Filament\n(b) Pili\n(c) Fimbriae\n(d) Polysome\nList-II\n(i) Elongated tubular structures made of a special protein\n(ii) Several ribosomes attached to a single mRNA\n(iii) Longest portion of the bacterial flagellum\n(iv) Small bristle-like fibres sprouting out of the cell","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The filament is the longest part of the flagellum, and pili are elongated tubular protein structures. Fimbriae are small bristle-like fibres, and a polysome is a chain of ribosomes on one mRNA. The usual trap is swapping the descriptions of pili and fimbriae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"While studying animal cells, Theodore Schwann reported a thin outer layer around the cells. This layer is known today as the:","image":"","options":["Cell wall","Middle lamella","Plasma membrane","Nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schwann's thin outer layer of animal cells is what we now call the plasma membrane. He regarded the cell wall as a feature unique to plant cells, so it cannot be the layer he saw in animal cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Within a chloroplast, chlorophyll pigments are present in the","image":"","options":["stroma","outer membrane","inner membrane","thylakoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll pigments are located in the thylakoid membranes. The stroma contains enzymes, DNA and ribosomes but not the pigments.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Pili are elongated tubular surface structures of bacteria made of a special protein.\nStatement II: Pili, like flagella, are the organs of motility in bacteria.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pili are indeed elongated tubular protein structures, so Statement I is correct. However, pili and fimbriae play no role in motility; only flagella move the bacterium, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Among prokaryotes, mycoplasma is the exception in lacking a cell wall around the cell membrane.\nStatement II: The two centrioles of a centrosome lie parallel to each other.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that all prokaryotes have a cell wall except mycoplasma, so Statement I is correct. The two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other, not parallel, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The 70S ribosome of a prokaryotic cell is formed by the association of which two subunits?","image":"","options":["60S and 40S","50S and 30S","40S and 30S","50S and 20S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Prokaryotic 70S ribosomes are made of a 50S large subunit and a 30S small subunit. The pair 60S and 40S belongs to eukaryotic 80S ribosomes, which is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In plant cells, the concentration of many ions is significantly higher inside the vacuole than in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II: The plant vacuole is bounded by a double membrane that allows ions to diffuse freely into it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ions do accumulate to higher concentrations inside the vacuole. This happens because the single-membrane tonoplast transports them against their concentration gradient, not because they diffuse freely through a double membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations would contain 70S ribosomes?\nCytoplasm of a bacterium; mitochondrial matrix; chloroplast stroma; surface of rough ER in a liver cell; cytoplasm of a cyanobacterium; free cytoplasmic ribosomes of an onion root cell","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four locations have 70S ribosomes: prokaryotes (bacteria and cyanobacteria), mitochondria and chloroplasts. Ribosomes on the RER and in the eukaryotic cytoplasm are 80S.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A student counts the individual microtubules seen in a transverse section of (P) the axoneme of a typical eukaryotic flagellum and (Q) a centriole. The correct counts for P and Q respectively are:","image":"","options":["11 and 9","18 and 27","20 and 27","20 and 9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The axoneme has 9 doublets (18 tubules) plus the central pair, giving 20 microtubules. A centriole has 9 triplets, giving 27 microtubules. The trap is counting doublets or triplets as single units, or forgetting the central pair.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist first saw and described a live cell?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown","Antonie Von Leeuwenhoek","Theodore Schwann","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT credits Antonie Von Leeuwenhoek with first seeing and describing a live cell. Robert Brown later discovered the nucleus, which is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is true for a secretory cell?","image":"","options":["Golgi apparatus is absent","Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER) is easily observed in the cell","Only Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER) is present","Secretory granules are formed in nucleus."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Secretory cells make large amounts of protein, so ribosome-studded RER is abundant and easy to see. These cells also have a well-developed Golgi apparatus that packages the secretions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is not correct?","image":"","options":["Robert Brown discovered the cell.","Schleiden and Schwann formulated the cell theory.","Virchow explained that cells are formed from pre-existing cells.","A unicellular organism carries out its life activities within a single cell."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Robert Hooke discovered the cell in 1665. Robert Brown discovered the nucleus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the plasma membrane, phospholipids are arranged so that their polar heads face the outer sides and their hydrophobic tails face the inner part of the bilayer.\nStatement II: Membrane proteins are classified as integral and peripheral on the basis of their role in active transport.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The head-out, tail-in arrangement shields the nonpolar tails from water, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the integral and peripheral classification depends on how easily the proteins can be extracted. Peripheral proteins lie on the surface, while integral proteins are partly or fully buried.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nuclear envelope is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Nuclear pores are formed by fusion of the two membranes of the envelope","RNA and protein molecules move through nuclear pores in both directions","The inner nuclear membrane bears ribosomes and is continuous with the Golgi apparatus","The outer nuclear membrane is usually continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"It is the outer membrane that is continuous with the ER and bears ribosomes. The inner membrane encloses the nucleoplasm and chromatin, so option 3 is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The secondary cell wall is formed on the inner side of the primary wall, towards the plasma membrane","Of the two chloroplast membranes, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable","Vesicles carrying material from the ER fuse with the concave trans face of the Golgi apparatus","The tonoplast transports ions into the vacuole against the concentration gradient, so their concentration in the vacuole exceeds that in the cytoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"ER-derived vesicles fuse with the convex cis, or forming, face of the Golgi. Materials then move towards the concave trans, or maturing, face, from which they are released. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mesosomes help in DNA replication and in the distribution of DNA to daughter cells in bacteria.\nReason (R): Mesosomes are formed by extensions of the bacterial cell wall into the cytoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mesosomes do help in DNA replication and in distributing DNA to daughter cells, so A is true. R is false because mesosomes are extensions of the plasma membrane, not the cell wall.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A described diagram of a cell structure, seen in transverse section, shows the following: nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin, each a triplet, with adjacent triplets linked. A central proteinaceous hub is joined to the peripheral tubules by radial spokes, giving a cartwheel appearance. Two such cylinders lie perpendicular to each other. The structure is:","image":"","options":["Axoneme of a cilium","Centriole","Basal body of a bacterial flagellum","Microfilament bundle of the cytoskeleton"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Nine peripheral triplets, a central hub and radial spokes in a cartwheel arrangement describe a centriole, and a centrosome usually has two of them lying perpendicular to each other. The axoneme has nine doublets plus a central pair (9+2), not triplets.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the bacterial cell envelope is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Although each envelope layer has a distinct function, the layers act together as a single protective unit","The division of bacteria into Gram positive and Gram negative is based on differences in the cell envelope and response to Gram's staining","The bacterial plasma membrane is structurally similar to that of eukaryotes","The glycocalyx is uniform in composition and thickness in all bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among bacteria, ranging from a loose slime layer to a thick capsule. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions attributed to mesosomes in prokaryotic cells?\n(i) Cell wall formation\n(ii) DNA replication\n(iii) Housing photosynthetic pigments\n(iv) Respiration\n(v) Secretion processes\n(vi) Attachment of bacteria to host tissues","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Four items are mesosome functions: cell wall formation, DNA replication, respiration and secretion. Pigments are held in chromatophores, and attachment to host tissues is a role of fimbriae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Of the two membranes of a chloroplast, the inner membrane is relatively less permeable.\nStatement II: The space enclosed by the thylakoid membrane is called the stroma.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The inner chloroplast membrane is indeed the less permeable one. However, the space enclosed by the thylakoid membrane is the lumen, while the stroma is the space bounded by the inner membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A sectional diagram of a chloroplast shows three labelled parts:\nX: stacks of flattened membranous sacs arranged like piles of coins\nY: flat tubules linking sacs of different stacks\nZ: the matrix enclosed by the inner membrane\nWhich of the following statements about this diagram is correct?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll pigments are located in Z","X are grana, the site of light reactions, and Z contains enzymes for synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins","Y are cristae that increase the surface area for photosynthesis","The lumen is the space between the outer and the inner chloroplast membranes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X are grana, where chlorophyll-bearing thylakoids carry out light reactions. Z is the stroma, which holds enzymes for carbohydrate and protein synthesis. Chlorophyll lies in the thylakoids rather than the stroma, Y are stroma lamellae, and the lumen is enclosed by the thylakoid membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In some prokaryotes such as cyanobacteria, pigments are held in membranous extensions of the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm. These structures are called:","image":"","options":["Thylakoids of chloroplasts","Chromatophores","Plasmids","Inclusion bodies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chromatophores are pigment-containing membranous extensions into the cytoplasm in prokaryotes like cyanobacteria. Prokaryotes have no chloroplasts, and inclusion bodies are reserve materials that are not bound by any membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A chromosome has its centromere situated close to one end, forming one extremely short arm and one very long arm. This chromosome is classified as:","image":"","options":["Metacentric","Sub-metacentric","Telocentric","Acrocentric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A centromere near the end, giving one very short and one very long arm, defines an acrocentric chromosome. Telocentric chromosomes have a strictly terminal centromere. Sub-metacentric chromosomes have the centromere only slightly away from the middle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every chromosome possesses a secondary constriction that gives it a satellite.\nStatement II: The centromere, or primary constriction, holds the two chromatids of a chromosome, and kinetochores lie on its sides.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Every chromosome has a primary constriction, the centromere, which holds the chromatids and bears kinetochores on its sides. Only a few chromosomes have a secondary constriction producing a satellite. The trap is mixing up the universal primary constriction with the occasional secondary one.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the plasma membrane are correct?\n(i) The quasi-fluid nature of lipids enables lateral movement of proteins within the bilayer.\n(ii) Membrane fluidity is important for endocytosis, secretion and cell division.\n(iii) The Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump moves ions along their concentration gradient without using ATP.\n(iv) Before the electron microscope era, chemical studies on human RBCs helped scientists deduce the possible structure of the membrane.\n(v) The ratio of protein to lipid is the same in the membranes of all cell types.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct, giving three. Statement (iii) is wrong because the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is active transport: it uses ATP and moves ions against the gradient. Statement (v) is wrong because the protein-to-lipid ratio varies considerably between cell types.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are functions of the plant cell wall?\n(i) Giving shape to the cell\n(ii) Protection from mechanical damage\n(iii) Protection from infection\n(iv) Helping in cell-to-cell interaction\n(v) Acting as a barrier to undesirable macromolecules\n(vi) Synthesis of ATP\n(vii) Synthesis of steroidal hormones","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The cell wall gives shape, protects against mechanical damage and infection, helps cell-to-cell interaction and blocks undesirable macromolecules, which makes five functions. ATP synthesis occurs in mitochondria, and steroidal hormones are made in the SER of animal cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about inclusion bodies of prokaryotic cells are correct?\n(i) They are bounded by a single membrane.\n(ii) They lie free in the cytoplasm.\n(iii) They store reserve material.\n(iv) Glycogen granules and cyanophycean granules are examples.\n(v) Gas vacuoles occur in all bacteria.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (ii), (iii) and (iv) are correct. Inclusion bodies are not bound by any membrane, so (i) is wrong. Gas vacuoles occur only in blue-green algae and in purple and green photosynthetic bacteria, not in all bacteria, so (v) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Ribosome in the cytoplasm of a eukaryotic cell\nB. Larger subunit of a prokaryotic ribosome\nC. Smaller subunit of a eukaryotic ribosome\nD. Ribosome in the mitochondrial matrix\nList-II\n(i) 40S\n(ii) 70S\n(iii) 50S\n(iv) 80S\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Cytoplasmic eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S, made of 60S and 40S subunits. Prokaryotic 70S ribosomes have 50S and 30S subunits, and mitochondria also contain 70S ribosomes. The trap is assuming that ribosomes inside a eukaryotic organelle are 80S.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Rough endoplasmic reticulum\n(B) Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\n(C) Golgi apparatus\n(D) Vacuole\nList-II\n(i) Bounded by tonoplast; may occupy up to 90% of plant cell volume\n(ii) Important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids\n(iii) Major site of lipid synthesis\n(iv) Continuous with the outer nuclear membrane; abundant in secretory cells\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"RER is continuous with the outer nuclear membrane and is extensive in cells that synthesise and secrete proteins. SER is the main site of lipid synthesis, the Golgi forms glycoproteins and glycolipids, and the vacuole is bounded by the tonoplast.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The approximate diameter of a human red blood cell is","image":"","options":["0.7 $\\mu m$","7.0 $\\mu m$","70 $\\mu m$","7.0 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Human RBCs are about 7.0 $\\mu m$ in diameter. The other options are off by factors of ten or more, or use the wrong unit.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following observations, if it were ever genuinely made, would contradict the cell theory as it is understood today?","image":"","options":["A mature mammalian erythrocyte that lacks a nucleus","An organism whose entire body consists of a single cell","An animal cell that has a plasma membrane but no cell wall","Living cells appearing spontaneously from non-living material without any pre-existing cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The modern cell theory states that all cells arise from pre-existing cells, so cells forming spontaneously from non-living matter would contradict it. Cells without a nucleus, unicellular organisms and cells without walls are all consistent with the theory, because it requires neither a nucleus nor a cell wall in every cell.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Middle lamella\n(B) Algal cell wall\n(C) Primary wall\n(D) Plasmodesmata\nList-II\n(I) Capable of growth in a young cell\n(II) Cytoplasmic connections between adjacent cells\n(III) Mainly calcium pectate\n(IV) Galactans and mannans\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The middle lamella is mainly calcium pectate. Algal walls contain galactans and mannans, the primary wall of a young cell can grow, and plasmodesmata connect neighbouring cytoplasms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A unicellular organism is considered a complete living unit because it is capable of:","image":"","options":["Independent existence and performing the essential functions of life","Forming tissues and organs from a single cell","Surviving only when attached to a host cell","Photosynthesis and cell wall formation in all cases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that unicellular organisms can (i) exist independently and (ii) perform all essential life functions. Anything less than a complete cell cannot ensure independent living.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Matthias Schleiden was a German zoologist who reported that animal cells have a thin outer layer.\nStatement II: Based on his studies of plant tissues, Theodore Schwann concluded that the presence of a cell wall is a unique character of plant cells.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schleiden was a German botanist who studied plants. It was Schwann, the zoologist, who described the outer layer of animal cells, so Statement I swaps their roles. Statement II correctly reports Schwann's conclusion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a mesophyll cell of a green leaf, the ribosomes in the chloroplast stroma and the ribosomes free in the cytoplasm are respectively:","image":"","options":["80S and 70S","70S and 80S","70S and 70S","80S and 80S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chloroplast ribosomes are 70S, which is smaller than the 80S ribosomes found in the cytoplasm of eukaryotic cells. A common trap is assuming that all ribosomes inside a eukaryotic cell are 80S.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a longitudinal section of a mitochondrion, the inner membrane folds inwards towards the matrix. What is the main role of these infoldings?","image":"","options":["They increase the surface area of the inner membrane","They store reserve food as starch granules","They connect the mitochondrion to the endoplasmic reticulum","They form the continuous limiting boundary of the organelle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The infoldings are called cristae, and they increase the surface area of the inner membrane. The continuous limiting boundary of the mitochondrion is formed by the outer membrane, not the cristae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Chromosome type)\nA. Metacentric\nB. Sub-metacentric\nC. Acrocentric\nD. Telocentric\nList-II (Arm feature)\nI. One extremely short and one very long arm\nII. Two equal arms\nIII. Centromere at the terminal end\nIV. One shorter and one longer arm\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A middle centromere gives equal arms (metacentric). A centromere slightly off-centre gives unequal arms (sub-metacentric). A centromere near the end gives one extremely short arm (acrocentric), and a terminal centromere means telocentric. The usual trap is confusing sub-metacentric with acrocentric.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A microorganism observed under the electron microscope shows the following features:\n(i) a cell wall surrounding the plasma membrane\n(ii) naked circular DNA with no nuclear envelope\n(iii) only 70S ribosomes, with no membrane-bound organelles\n(iv) pigment-containing membranous extensions of the plasma membrane into the cytoplasm\n(v) cyanophycean granules and gas vacuoles in the cytoplasm\nThe organism is most likely:","image":"","options":["Mycoplasma","Chlamydomonas","A cyanobacterium","Euglena"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Naked DNA, 70S ribosomes and no membrane-bound organelles mark it as a prokaryote, and the cell wall rules out mycoplasma. Chromatophores, cyanophycean granules and gas vacuoles indicate a blue-green alga (cyanobacterium). Chlamydomonas and Euglena are eukaryotes with membrane-bound chloroplasts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of these is not a eukaryote?","image":"","options":["Euglena","Anabaena","Spirogyra","Agaricus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Anabaena is a cyanobacterium (blue-green alga), so it is a prokaryote. Euglena is a protist, Spirogyra is a green alga and Agaricus is a fungus; all three are eukaryotes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following features is common to prokaryotes and many eukaryotes?","image":"","options":["Chromatin material","Cell wall present","Nuclear membrane present","Membranes bound sub-cellular organelles present"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Most prokaryotes have a cell wall, and so do many eukaryotes such as plants and fungi. Prokaryotes lack a nuclear membrane, membrane-bound organelles and histone-associated chromatin.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following contributions in correct chronological order:\nP. Virchow explains that new cells arise from pre-existing cells\nQ. Leeuwenhoek sees and describes a live cell\nR. Schwann proposes that animal and plant bodies are made of cells and products of cells\nS. Robert Brown describes the nucleus\nT. Schleiden reports that all plants are composed of different kinds of cells","image":"","options":["S, Q, T, R, P","Q, S, T, R, P","Q, R, T, S, P","Q, S, P, T, R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Leeuwenhoek came first. Brown described the nucleus in 1831, followed by Schleiden (1838), Schwann (1839) and finally Virchow (1855).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are found in the mitochondrial matrix?\nSingle circular DNA molecule; a few RNA molecules; 80S ribosomes; 70S ribosomes; chlorophyll; components required for protein synthesis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The matrix contains circular DNA, a few RNA molecules, 70S ribosomes and the components needed for protein synthesis, which makes four. 80S ribosomes are cytoplasmic eukaryotic ribosomes, and chlorophyll occurs only in chloroplast thylakoids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are considered part of the endomembrane system?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Lysosomes, Peroxisomes, Vacuoles, Chloroplasts, Ribosomes","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles belong to the endomembrane system, because their functions are coordinated. Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes are membrane-bound but not coordinated with these organelles. Ribosomes lack a membrane altogether.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The largest isolated single cell is the","image":"","options":["nerve cell of a human","egg of an ostrich","tracheid of a plant","egg of a hen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names the ostrich egg as the largest isolated single cell. Nerve cells are among the longest cells, but they are not the largest isolated cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is described in NCERT as the largest isolated single cell?","image":"","options":["Human nerve cell","Egg of an ostrich","Human red blood cell","Tracheid of a plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ostrich egg is the largest isolated single cell. Nerve cells are among the longest cells, but they are not the largest isolated single cell. That difference is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The widely accepted fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed by:","image":"","options":["Schleiden and Schwann (1838)","Watson and Crick (1953)","Singer and Nicolson (1972)","George Palade (1953)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Singer and Nicolson proposed the fluid mosaic model in 1972. Palade is linked with ribosomes, and Schleiden and Schwann with cell theory.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The single membrane that bounds the vacuole of a plant cell is called the","image":"","options":["plasmalemma","tonoplast","cisterna","nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The vacuole is enclosed by a single membrane called the tonoplast. The plasmalemma is the outer cell membrane, and cisternae are the sacs of the ER and Golgi.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes across the plasma membrane require the expenditure of ATP?\n(i) Simple diffusion of a neutral solute from higher to lower concentration\n(ii) Osmosis\n(iii) Carrier protein-assisted movement of a polar molecule from higher to lower concentration\n(iv) Working of the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump\n(v) Uptake of an ion from lower to higher concentration","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only (iv) and (v) move substances against a concentration gradient, so only they need ATP. Carrier-assisted movement down a gradient is still passive, even though a protein is involved.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is not true for plasma membrane?","image":"","options":["It is present in both plant and animal cell","Lipid is present as a bilayer in it","Proteins are present integrated as well as loosely associated with the lipid bilayer","Carbohydrate is never found in it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The plasma membrane does contain carbohydrates, mainly as glycoproteins and glycolipids on its outer surface. So the claim that carbohydrate is never found in it is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A colourless plastid in a seed cell is found to store proteins. This plastid is a/an:","image":"","options":["Amyloplast","Elaioplast","Aleuroplast","Chromoplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Leucoplasts are colourless plastids that store nutrients. Aleuroplasts store proteins, amyloplasts store starch and elaioplasts store oils and fats. Chromoplasts are not colourless, since they contain carotenoids.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following locations contain 70S ribosomes?\n(i) Cytoplasm of a bacterial cell\n(ii) Matrix of a mitochondrion\n(iii) Stroma of a chloroplast\n(iv) Surface of rough ER in a plant cell\n(v) Cytoplasm of PPLO\n(vi) Free in the cytoplasm of an animal cell","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bacteria and PPLO are prokaryotes with 70S ribosomes, and the mitochondrial matrix and chloroplast stroma also carry 70S ribosomes. Ribosomes on the rough ER and free in the eukaryotic cytoplasm are 80S.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The membrane of the human erythrocyte contains approximately","image":"","options":["40 per cent protein and 52 per cent lipids","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipids","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent carbohydrates","90 per cent lipids and 10 per cent protein"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The human RBC membrane has about 52% protein and 40% lipid, so protein exceeds lipid. The common trap is reversing the two values.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is correct:","image":"","options":["Cells of all living organisms have a nucleus.","Both animal and plant cells have a well defined cell wall.","In prokaryotes, there are no membrane bound organelles.","Cells are formed de novo from abiotic materials."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles and a true nucleus. The other statements are false: prokaryotes lack a nucleus, animal cells have no cell wall, and cells arise from pre-existing cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of leucoplast) with List-II (stored material):\nList-I\nA. Amyloplast\nB. Elaioplast\nC. Aleuroplast\nList-II\n(i) Proteins\n(ii) Oils and fats\n(iii) Starch","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amyloplasts store starch, as in potato. Elaioplasts store oils and fats, and aleuroplasts store proteins. Students often swap elaioplasts and aleuroplasts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Polar molecules require a carrier protein to cross the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): The interior of the lipid bilayer is formed by nonpolar hydrophobic tails.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The phospholipid tails point inward and form a nonpolar core. Polar molecules cannot pass through this core by simple diffusion, so they need carrier proteins. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In the human erythrocyte membrane, what are the approximate proportions of protein and lipid?","image":"","options":["40 per cent protein and 52 per cent lipid","52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipid","60 per cent protein and 30 per cent lipid","25 per cent protein and 75 per cent lipid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the human RBC membrane has about 52 per cent protein and 40 per cent lipid. The common trap is swapping the two values.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Radial spoke of axoneme\n(b) Hub\n(c) Basal body\n(d) Central sheath\nList-II\n(i) Proteinaceous central part of the proximal region of a centriole\n(ii) Centriole-like structure from which a cilium emerges\n(iii) Encloses the central pair of microtubules of the axoneme\n(iv) Joins the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the axoneme, radial spokes connect the central sheath, which encloses the central pair, to one tubule of each peripheral doublet. The hub is the proteinaceous centre of the proximal region of a centriole. Cilia and flagella arise from basal bodies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"An electron micrograph of a transverse section of a eukaryotic flagellum shows a ring of peripheral doublet microtubules. It also shows a central pair of microtubules enclosed in a central sheath. Each peripheral doublet is joined to the central sheath by one radial spoke. How many radial spokes are seen in the section?","image":"","options":["2","9","11","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The axoneme has a 9+2 arrangement. One tubule of each of the nine peripheral doublets is connected to the central sheath by a radial spoke, giving nine radial spokes. The number 18 counts individual peripheral microtubules, not spokes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Ribosomes were first observed as dense particles under the electron microscope by which scientist?","image":"","options":["Camillo Golgi","George Palade","Robert Brown","Rudolf Virchow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"George Palade (1953) first saw ribosomes as dense granular particles under the electron microscope. Camillo Golgi described the Golgi bodies, Robert Brown the nucleus, and Virchow stated that cells arise from pre-existing cells.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following membrane-bound organelles is NOT included in the endomembrane system of a eukaryotic cell?","image":"","options":["Golgi complex","Peroxisome","Vacuole","Endoplasmic reticulum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The endomembrane system groups the ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles because their functions are coordinated. Peroxisomes, mitochondria and chloroplasts are membrane-bound, but their functions are not coordinated with these organelles, so they are excluded.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about cell walls is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The cell wall is a non-living rigid covering outside the plasma membrane of plants and fungi.","In plants other than algae, the cell wall consists of cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.","All prokaryotes possess a cell wall surrounding the cell membrane.","In bacteria, the cell wall determines cell shape and prevents the cell from bursting or collapsing."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mycoplasma is the exception among prokaryotes because it lacks a cell wall, so the statement that all prokaryotes have one is wrong. The other three statements match NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Integral proteins\nB. Peripheral proteins\nC. Phospholipids\nD. Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump\nList-II\nI. Arranged as a bilayer in the membrane\nII. Lie on the surface of the membrane\nIII. Partially or totally buried in the membrane\nIV. Moves ions against their concentration gradient using ATP\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Integral proteins are embedded partly or fully in the membrane, while peripheral proteins sit on its surface. Phospholipids form the bilayer, and the Na$^+$/K$^+$ pump is ATP-driven active transport. The usual trap is interchanging the two protein types.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cells that are actively carrying out protein synthesis have larger and more numerous nucleoli.\nReason (R): The nucleolus is a site of active ribosomal RNA synthesis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Protein synthesis requires many ribosomes, which are made of rRNA and proteins. The nucleolus produces rRNA, so cells making lots of protein need bigger and more nucleoli. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Microbodies are membrane-bound minute vesicles containing various enzymes, present in both plant and animal cells.\nStatement II: In a single human cell, approximately two metre long DNA is distributed among its twenty-three chromosomes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I matches NCERT's description of microbodies. In Statement II, the 2 m of DNA is distributed among 46 chromosomes (23 pairs), not 23, so it is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The cytoskeleton is made of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments present in the cytoplasm.\nStatement II: The cytoskeleton is a network of membrane-bound lipid tubules that mainly synthesises steroid hormones.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cytoskeleton is a network of proteinaceous filaments that provides mechanical support, motility and maintenance of cell shape. Statement II describes features of the smooth ER, not the cytoskeleton.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following:\nColumn I: (a) Cristae, (b) Cisternae, (c) Thylakoids\nColumn II: (i) Flat membranous sacs in stroma, (ii) Infoldings in mitochondria, (iii) Disc-shaped sacs in Golgi apparatus","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane. Cisternae are the disc-shaped sacs of the Golgi apparatus. Thylakoids are flat membranous sacs in the chloroplast stroma.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 (Exercise Q3) (converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In eukaryotic plant cells, ribosomes are found not only in the cytoplasm but also inside chloroplasts and mitochondria.\nStatement II: Prokaryotic cells lack all types of organelles, so no protein-synthesising particles are present in them.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct, since ribosomes occur in the cytoplasm, on rough ER, and within chloroplasts and mitochondria. Statement II is wrong because prokaryotes lack only membrane-bound organelles; they do have ribosomes (70S), which synthesise proteins.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nuclear envelope has two parallel membranes with a space between them called the perinuclear space. According to NCERT, what is the approximate width of this space?","image":"","options":["1 to 5 nm","100 to 500 nm","10 to 50 nm","0.5 to 1.0 µm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electron microscopy shows a perinuclear space of 10 to 50 nm between the two membranes of the nuclear envelope. The value 0.5 to 1.0 µm is the diameter of Golgi cisternae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A common characteristic feature of plant sieve tube cells and most of mammalian erythrocytes is","image":"","options":["Absence of mitochondria","Presence of cell wall","Presence of haemoglobin","Absence of nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Mature sieve tube elements and most mammalian red blood cells both lose their nucleus during differentiation. Only plant cells have a cell wall, and haemoglobin is found only in erythrocytes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The nucleolus is primarily a site for:","image":"","options":["Active ribosomal RNA synthesis","Synthesis of steroid hormones","Packaging of glycoproteins","Synthesis of histone proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The nucleolus is a site of active rRNA synthesis. Steroid hormones are made in SER, and glycoproteins are formed and packaged in the Golgi apparatus.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In a chloroplast, which structures are the flat membranous tubules that connect the thylakoids of different grana?","image":"","options":["Cristae","Cisternae","Stroma lamellae","Plasmodesmata"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Stroma lamellae are the flat membranous tubules that link thylakoids belonging to different grana. Cristae are mitochondrial infoldings, and cisternae are Golgi or ER sacs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the nuclear envelope is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Nuclear pores are formed by the fusion of the two membranes of the envelope","The outer nuclear membrane usually remains continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum","The inner nuclear membrane bears ribosomes on its surface facing the nucleoplasm","Mature erythrocytes of many mammals and sieve tube cells of vascular plants lack a nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ribosomes are borne on the outer nuclear membrane, which is continuous with the ER, not on the inner membrane. The inner membrane simply encloses the nucleoplasm and chromatin. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A bacterial flagellum has three parts: filament, hook and basal body. Which part is the longest and extends from the cell surface to the outside?","image":"","options":["Basal body","Hook","Filament","Pilus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The filament is the longest part of the bacterial flagellum and projects outward from the cell surface. The hook and basal body are shorter parts that lie near the cell. A pilus is a separate surface structure, not a part of the flagellum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"The inner mitochondrial membrane forms infoldings called cristae. What is the main significance of these infoldings?","image":"","options":["They divide the matrix into separate compartments for DNA storage","They increase the surface area of the inner membrane","They connect the mitochondrion with the endoplasmic reticulum","They form the outer limiting boundary of the organelle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cristae fold inward towards the matrix and increase the surface area of the inner membrane. The continuous limiting boundary is formed by the outer membrane, not by the cristae.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"In animal cells, lipid-like steroidal hormones are synthesised mainly in the","image":"","options":["rough endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","smooth endoplasmic reticulum","lysosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The SER lacks ribosomes and is the major site of lipid synthesis, which includes steroid hormones in animal cells. The RER is linked mainly with protein synthesis and secretion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are membrane-bound organelles of a eukaryotic cell?\nEndoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Ribosome, Lysosome, Centrosome, Mitochondrion, Microbody, Vacuole","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"ER, Golgi complex, lysosome, mitochondrion, microbody and vacuole are membrane-bound, giving 6. Ribosomes and centrosomes are non-membrane bound.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell type) with List-II (shape):\nList-I\nA. Red blood cell\nB. White blood cell\nC. Columnar epithelial cell\nD. Nerve cell\nList-II\nI. Amoeboid\nII. Branched and long\nIII. Round and biconcave\nIV. Long and narrow","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"RBCs are round and biconcave, WBCs are amoeboid, columnar epithelial cells are long and narrow, and nerve cells are branched and long. Cell shape varies with the function the cell performs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following structures is present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?","image":"","options":["Golgi complex","Mitochondrion","Ribosome","Nuclear envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ribosomes are non-membrane bound organelles found in all cells. Prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles and a nuclear membrane.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the smallest cell, and what is its approximate length?","image":"","options":["Bacterium, about 3 $\\mu m$","Mycoplasma, about 0.3 $\\mu m$","Human red blood cell, about 7.0 $\\mu m$","Virus, about 0.3 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mycoplasmas are the smallest cells, only about 0.3 $\\mu m$ long. Viruses are not cells, and bacteria are roughly ten times larger.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The axoneme of a cilium has nine radial spokes.\nReason (R): The central sheath around the central pair of microtubules is joined to one tubule of each of the nine peripheral doublets by a radial spoke.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each of the nine peripheral doublets is linked to the central sheath by one radial spoke. This is exactly why there are nine radial spokes, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about mitochondria and plastids is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A typical mitochondrion has an average diameter of about 0.5 µm and a length of 1.0–4.1 µm","Each cell of Chlamydomonas has 20–40 chloroplasts, like a mesophyll cell","The inner and outer mitochondrial membranes divide its lumen into an outer and an inner aqueous compartment","Plastids occur in all plant cells and also in euglenoides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlamydomonas has only one chloroplast per cell, whereas mesophyll cells have 20–40. All the other statements match NCERT's descriptions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plastid) with List-II (stored material or pigment):\nList-I\nA. Amyloplast\nB. Elaioplast\nC. Aleuroplast\nD. Chromoplast\nList-II\nI. Proteins\nII. Carotene and xanthophylls\nIII. Starch\nIV. Oils and fats","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Amyloplasts store starch (for example in potato), elaioplasts store oils and fats, and aleuroplasts store proteins. Chromoplasts hold fat-soluble carotenoids such as carotene and xanthophylls. Students often swap elaioplast and aleuroplast.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Who proposed the fluid mosaic model of plasma membrane?","image":"","options":["Benda","Schleiden and Schwann","Singer and Nicolson","Robert Brown"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Singer and Nicolson proposed the fluid mosaic model in 1972. It describes the membrane as a quasi-fluid lipid bilayer with proteins embedded in it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 8 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms.\nReason (R): Anything less than a complete structure of a cell does not ensure independent living.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Unicellular organisms can exist independently and perform all essential life functions. Since nothing less than a whole cell can do this, R directly justifies A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements comparing the axoneme of a cilium with a centriole is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The axoneme has nine peripheral doublets and a central pair of microtubules enclosed by a central sheath","In the axoneme, nine radial spokes connect the central sheath to one tubule of each peripheral doublet","In a centriole, each of the nine evenly spaced peripheral fibrils of tubulin is a doublet, joined to a central hub by radial spokes","The two centrioles of a centrosome lie perpendicular to each other and can form the basal body of a cilium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each peripheral fibril of a centriole is a triplet, not a doublet. Doublets belong to the 9+2 axoneme of cilia and flagella, while the centriole has a cartwheel of nine triplets around a proteinaceous hub.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the plasma membrane, phospholipids are arranged so that their polar heads face the outer sides of the bilayer and their hydrophobic tails face the interior.\nReason (R): This arrangement keeps the nonpolar hydrocarbon tails protected from the aqueous environment.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The polar heads interact with water on both faces, while the nonpolar tails are tucked inside the bilayer. The orientation exists precisely to shield the tails from water, so R explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-191","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":191,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"A secretory glycoprotein is synthesised by ribosomes on the endoplasmic reticulum and finally released outside the cell. Which sequence correctly traces its path?","image":"","options":["RER → transport vesicle → trans face of Golgi → cis face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane","RER → transport vesicle → cis face of Golgi → trans face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane","SER → transport vesicle → cis face of Golgi → trans face of Golgi → lysosome → plasma membrane","Cis face of Golgi → RER → trans face of Golgi → secretory vesicle → plasma membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The protein is made on RER and carried in vesicles that fuse with the cis (forming) face of the Golgi. It is modified, including glycosylation, while moving to the trans (maturing) face, then packaged for secretion. Reversing the cis–trans direction or starting from SER is the usual trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-192","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell: The Unit of Life","qno":192,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell: The Unit of Life","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the plasma membrane is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The ratio of protein to lipid in the membrane is the same in all cell types","The ability of proteins to move laterally within the bilayer is measured as membrane fluidity","The fluid nature of the membrane is important for endocytosis, secretion and formation of intercellular junctions","Besides phospholipids and proteins, the membrane also contains cholesterol and carbohydrates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the protein to lipid ratio varies considerably among cell types, so the first statement is wrong. The other three correctly describe membrane fluidity, the functions it supports, and the membrane's components.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8 · Cell: The Unit of Life","difficulty":"hard"}]});
